WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.261
A practical secure C baseline starts with
input and sizes.

2
00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:10.574
Treat input as untrusted until validated.

3
00:00:10.654 --> 00:00:14.887
Track buffer capacity and current length.

4
00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:18.494
Keep string termination explicit.

5
00:00:18.574 --> 00:00:26.335
Validate numeric values before they
control allocation, indexing, copying,

6
00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:27.943
or loop bounds.

7
00:00:28.023 --> 00:00:32.609
Manage ownership and lifetime
deliberately.

8
00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:39.038
Know which object a pointer refers to, how
long it remains valid,

9
00:00:39.118 --> 00:00:46.996
who owns dynamically allocated memory, and
which path releases each resource.

10
00:00:47.076 --> 00:00:52.837
Keep cleanup behavior clear on success and
failure paths.

11
00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:55.739
Avoid fragile assumptions.

12
00:00:55.819 --> 00:01:03.344
Keep format strings under program control,
handle truncation explicitly,

13
00:01:03.424 --> 00:01:11.655
avoid undefined behavior, and review
compiler and platform assumptions when code

14
00:01:11.735 --> 00:01:16.203
needs to be portable or
security-sensitive.

15
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:19.810
Use tooling as part of the workflow.

16
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:26.945
Compiler warnings, static analysis,
sanitizers, dependency review,

17
00:01:27.025 --> 00:01:31.846
and hardening options help find issues
earlier.

18
00:01:31.926 --> 00:01:40.391
They do not replace careful design, but
they make disciplined review more effective.

19
00:01:40.471 --> 00:01:44.704
Finally, test and review systematically.

20
00:01:44.784 --> 00:01:52.427
Exercise edge cases, oversized input,
partial reads, allocation failures,

21
00:01:52.507 --> 00:01:56.387
malformed records, and cleanup paths.

22
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:02.581
Review data flow, memory flow, resource
ownership, logging,

23
00:02:02.661 --> 00:02:06.423
and failure behavior before release.

24
00:02:06.503 --> 00:02:12.500
Secure C programming is a repeatable
engineering practice.
